paragraphs.text:
If an oscillating system with resistance receives only one initial push, its free oscillations usually decay gradually because of dissipation.
To sustain the oscillations, mechanical energy must continually be replenished from an energy source.
Forced oscillations are driven by an externally prescribed force that varies with time;

At the operating position marked in the source book's figure, a tooth presses against the sloping surface of pallet $b$, giving the pendulum a push to the left.
After the pendulum passes through its equilibrium position, pallet $b$ then disengages from that tooth;
the other pallet, $a$, interacts with another tooth during the other half-cycle, giving the pendulum a push in the opposite direction.

For an externally prescribed periodic driving force, the frequency of the forced oscillations equals the driving frequency in the steady periodic response discussed here.
The amplitude depends not only on the amplitude of the external force but also on the driving frequency, the system's natural properties, and damping;
at first, gradually decaying transient oscillations may also be superposed on the response.

Free oscillations receive no continuing supply of mechanical energy and gradually decay when damping is present;
ideal undamped free oscillations can retain their amplitude, so "no decay" alone cannot distinguish free oscillations from self-oscillations.
The energy and amplitude of free oscillations are determined by the initial conditions. This differs from self-oscillations, which receive a continuing energy supply and approach a stable operating state.

Looking back at the periodic mechanical oscillations discussed in this chapter, we need to understand not only position and velocity at any instant, but also quantities such as amplitude, period, and frequency that characterize the process as a whole.
We can also study the position and velocity at any instant of a spacecraft or planet moving under gravitational attraction;
studying oscillations does not mean abandoning these quantities of motion. It means focusing on the common patterns of repeated motion.

